2026-10-16T03:40:49.9331214Z   ISSUE_NUMBER: 55936
2026-10-16T03:40:49.9331535Z   ISSUE_TITLE: Cloudflare Workers AI: tool calls fail with 400 (content null on tool-call turns + array/string mix)
2026-10-16T03:40:49.9343942Z   ISSUE_BODY: Tool calls fail with HTTP 400 on Cloudflare Workers AI's OpenAI-compatible endpoint. Plain chat works (streaming included); the first turn where the model calls a tool fails and the turn cannot continue, so agentic use of the provider is impossible.

Root cause is the same as #51661: the assistant turn that carries `tool_calls` is serialised with `content: null` (deliberate, `packages/ai/src/protocols/openai-chat.ts:484`), and Cloudflare's validator requires a string. The Cloudflare-specific report #30381 was auto-closed by the staleness bot while still reproducing, so this is filed fresh for that path.

### Plugins

none

### OpenCode version

2.0.30

### Steps to reproduce

1. Configure a custom provider: OpenAI-compatible endpoint `https://api.cloudflare.com/client/v4/accounts/{ACCOUNT_ID}/ai/v1`, model e.g. `@cf/openai/gpt-oss-120b`. Same for `qwen3.8-27b`, `llama-4-scout-17b`.
2. Send any prompt that triggers a tool call, e.g. `use the shell tool to run 'echo hello'`.
3. The tool-call turn returns 400.

### Screenshot and/or share link

No share link (the provider credential is account-scoped). Failing request shape and error are below.

### Operating System

Linux x86_64 (Kali)

### Terminal

xterm-256color (fish)

### Error (verbatim)

```
AiError: Bad input: Error: oneOf at '/' not met, 0 matches: required properties at '/' are 'prompt',
Type mismatch of '/messages/0/content', 'array' not in 'string',
Type mismatch of '/messages/1/content', 'array' not in 'string',
Type mismatch of '/messages/2/content', 'string' not in 'null',
required properties at '/messages/2' are 'role,content', ...
```

Two distinct mismatches in one request: `content` as an **array** on some messages, and `null` on the assistant tool-call turn.

### Minimal proof

Captured the exact outbound body on the tool-call turn:

```
system(str), user(str),
assistant(content: null, tool_calls: [...], reasoning_content: "..."),
tool(str, tool_call_id), user(str)
```

(the request also carries `max_completion_tokens`, `store`, `stream`, `stream_options`, `tools`).

Single-variable replay against the same endpoint:

- verbatim body → **400**
- only `content: null` changed to `content: ""` → **200**

Nothing else differs. #41210 (merged) fixed only the empty-content branch (neither text nor tool calls → `""`); the tool-call branch still emits `null`.

### Interventions tried

| Intervention | Result |
| --- | --- |
| three models (`gpt-oss-120b`, `qwen3.8-27b`, `llama-4-scout-17b`) | chat 200, tools 400 |
| direct `/accounts/{id}/ai/v1/chat/completions` | 400 |
| AI Gateway `/compat/chat/completions` | 400 |
| bundled `cloudflare-workers-ai` provider runtime | 400 |
| `/ai/v1/responses` | GPT-OSS-only and non-streaming per docs; the runtime sends chat-shaped tools → rejected |
| provider config (`headers`, `extraBody`, …) | no request-body hook exposed |
| plugin | no request-body hook in v2.0.30 |

`transformRequestBody` exists as an internal AI SDK provider property but is not in the OpenAI-compatible provider settings schema, so a user has no supported way to change outgoing messages.

### Workaround (shim — not a fix)

A one-file loopback HTTP proxy in front of the endpoint that (a) rewrites `content: null`/absent to `""` and normalises array content to a string, and (b) injects the provider credential from a file per request so the client config holds no secret. Point `baseURL` at it; tool calling then works end to end for every model, streaming included, and upstream 4xx is relayed unchanged rather than masked as a 502.

Costs: an extra always-on process; it is an unauthenticated credential-injecting proxy (loopback-only, anything that reaches it can spend the account's quota); it hides the defect so regressions go unnoticed; it must be retired once fixed.

### Scope

- Trigger: any history containing an assistant turn with `tool_calls`. Plain chat is unaffected.
- Providers: every OpenAI-compatible validator that requires string content (Cloudflare observed; class is wider, cf. #41165).
- Not endpoint- or model-specific: all three transport paths and all models tested reproduce it.

### Proposed upstream change (tests, gates)

- Unit: serialise a history with an assistant `tool_calls` turn and assert every emitted message has `typeof content === 'string'` or no `content` key. Today `null` passes silently.
- Contract: add a strict-validator fixture (or a recorded real provider) to the provider matrix, so `openai-compatible` runtimes are tested against a validator and not a permissive stub.
- Gate: refuse to emit `content: null`; omit the key when a turn has `tool_calls`.
- Change control: expose a documented request-body transform (config or plugin hook) so adapting a strict provider does not require a sidecar.

### Affected users

Every OpenCode user doing tool work against a strict OpenAI-compatible provider; for Cloudflare Workers AI that is 100% of tool calls for every model — a hard blocker. This is Workers AI's second report (#30381) plus a relay report (#41165); a tracker search for `'string' not in 'null'` would refine the count.

### Discussion

Strictly, OpenAI tolerates `null` content on an assistant message that carries `tool_calls`, so the `null` is not wrong per spec — but validators that require a string are common, every other client we compared emits `""` or omits the key, and `""` satisfies both, so the cheap non-breaking fix is client-side. On Cloudflare this cannot be fixed by the user at all. The array/string mix in the same error means the request is not even self-consistent, so normalising message content once at serialisation would fix both.

### References

- #51661 (same root cause), #30381 (closed, still reproducible), #41165, #41210 (merged), #30589 / #36850 (closed)
- Cloudflare Workers AI — OpenAI compatibility: https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers-ai/configuration/open-ai-compatibility/
- Cloudflare Workers AI models: https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers-ai/models/

